**TypeScript Version:**  4.0.2

<!-- Search terms you tried before logging this (so others can find this issue more easily) -->
**Search Terms:** default generic conditional erased constrained

**Code**
```ts
// Some somewhat advanced conditional types
type UnionToIntersection<U> = (U extends any ? (k: U) => void : never) extends (k: infer I) => void ? I : never;
type IsNullable<T> = UnionToIntersection<T> extends T & null & undefined ? T : never;
declare function onlyNullables<T>(v: IsNullable<T>): void;

// Function modeled after querySelector<E extends Element = Element>(selectors: string): E | null;
declare function defaultGeneric<E extends number = number>(unrelatedInput: number): E | null;

let normalType = defaultGeneric(1); // GOOD: defaultGeneric<number>

onlyNullables(defaultGeneric(2)); // BAD: defaultGeneric<never>
```

**Expected behavior:**
`defaultGeneric` should default to `defaultGeneric<number>` in all positions.

**Actual behavior:**
`defaultGeneric` becomes `<number>` in some positions and `<never>` in others.

I believe the compiler is misinterpreting the constraints of the function, which makes it think it needs to be `never` instead of using the default `number`.  That is, the output type is constraining the generic type, which leads to an error further down the road.  I'm not sure if there is an easy fix, as outputs being used to constrain generics is a pretty common case (though technically incorrect), and the compiler would have to "peek-ahead" some unknown distance to know that this constraint would cause an error.

